      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Berlin opened its first public museum, the Altes Museum, in 1830, making the city a pioneer in sharing historic collections.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitberlin.de/en/museum-island-in-berlin",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Since its spectacular 2009 reopening, the Altes Museum now displays selected Egyptian, papyrus, prehistory, and classical antiquities.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitberlin.de/en/museum-island-in-berlin",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Museumsinsel U5 station opened on July 9 2021, featuring a single central platform 16 meters below street level under the Spree.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museumsinsel_(Berlin_U-Bahn)",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Construction froze the soil by pumping a −37 °C calcium chloride solution into 95 holes, a technique also used at Brandenburger Tor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
